    "result_text": "The full suite is green (BUILD SUCCESS, 1 test class / 1 test, 0 failures).\n\n## Summary\n\nI implemented the disposable-domain blocklist rejection in the owner-creation flow (`POST /api/owners`).\n\nChanges to `OwnerRestControllerV1.java`:\n\n1. **Blocklist constant** \u2014 added `DISPOSABLE_EMAIL_DOMAINS = {\"mailinator.com\", \"tempmail.com\", \"guerrillamail.com\"}`.\n\n2. **Domain check helper** \u2014 `emailDomainIsBlocked(email)` extracts the part after the final `@` and checks it against the blocklist.\n\n3. **Wired into `normalizeNewOwner`** \u2014 right after the email is normalised (lower-cased), a blocked domain now causes the method to return `false`, which `addOwner` already maps to `400 Bad Request`. This slots in alongside the existing field-level rejections (blank address, invalid telephone, bad postcode, future registration date), so the domain comparison runs against the canonical lower-cased email.\n\nThis is the natural place: the spec's rule is a field-level validation on the create endpoint, and `normalizeNewOwner` is the documented home for exactly those `400`-producing checks. The new acceptance test `errorRejectsDisposableDomain` passes, and the full suite is green.",
